  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Impulse",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the one-sided Laplace transform of $\\delta(t)$ by using the $0^-$ convention to include the impulse at the origin and convert it into the constant $s$-domain value $1$.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Impulse",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Laplace Transform of the Unit Impulse",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the one-sided definition as your starting template: $\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt$, where the integral starts at $0^-$ rather than $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For impulse calculations, the key cue is the sifting identity: the integral of $\\delta(t)$ against a test function returns the function value at zero, and the $0^-$ lower limit keeps this rule aligned with one-sided causal setup.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\\\{\\\\delta(t)\\\\}=\\\\int_{0^-}^{\\\\infty}\\\\delta(t)e^{-st}\\\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the sifting step directly: $\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}\\delta(t)e^{-st}\\,dt$ collapses to $e^{-s\\cdot 0}$, because the sampled point is $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\\\{\\\\delta(t)\\\\}=e^{-s\\\\cdot 0}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So this section’s result is $\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{\\delta(t)\\}=1$, and keeping $0^-$ is the boundary rule that preserves origin impulses in later shifted-impulse and signal-composition steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Impulse",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Applying Sifting to the One-Sided Transform of $\\delta(t)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{\\delta(t)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The goal here is to compute a concrete one-sided impulse transform by direct substitution, starting from the definition of the operation and then applying the sifting rule at the impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\\\{\\\\delta(t)\\\\}=\\\\int_{0^-}^{\\\\infty}\\\\delta(t)e^{-st}dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now recognize the cue: this is an impulse multiplied by a smooth test function, so use sifting. With $f(t)=e^{-st}$, the term $\\delta(t)f(t)$ evaluates to $f(0)$ inside the one-sided interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\int_{0^-}^{\\\\infty}\\\\delta(t)e^{-st}dt=e^{-s\\cdot 0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since the lower limit is $0^-$, the origin impulse is included in the one-sided interval, so this sampled value is retained. Simplify to $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\\\{\\\\delta(t)\\\\}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Impulse",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Including the Origin Impulse with the $0^-$ Lower Limit",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Show why using $\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}$ keeps $\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{\\delta(t)\\}=1$, while $\\int_{0^+}^{\\infty}$ would omit the origin impulse.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use both one-sided candidates so the boundary rule is explicit before evaluating the transform value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\int_{0^-}^{\\\\infty}\\\\delta(t)e^{-st}dt=1,\\quad \\int_{0^+}^{\\\\infty}\\\\delta(t)e^{-st}dt=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For one-sided transforms, the convention rule is: use the lower limit $0^-$ when an impulse or jump at the origin must be included in the signal interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\int_{0^-}^{\\\\infty}\\\\delta(t)e^{-st}dt=e^{-s\\cdot 0}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Only the $0^-$ interval samples the boundary impulse at $t=0$, so it returns the sifting contribution; the $0^+$ interval starts strictly after the impulse and contributes $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\int_{0^+}^{\\\\infty}\\\\delta(t)e^{-st}dt=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This topic’s core convention is therefore operational: with one-sided transforms, include the origin impulse via $0^-$ so that $\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{\\delta(t)\\}=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Impulse",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Transforming Scaled Unit Impulses at the Origin",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{5\\delta(t)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the same one-sided impulse setup as before, now with an amplitude coefficient: treat $x(t)=5\\delta(t)$ and keep the $0^-$ lower limit convention.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{5\\delta(t)\\}=\\\\int_{0^-}^{\\\\infty}5\\delta(t)e^{-st}dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the sifting rule and pull out the constant $5$ from the integral, since constants are linear under integration.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "5\\\\int_{0^-}^{\\\\infty}\\delta(t)e^{-st}dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the same impulse-at-the-origin evaluation as earlier: $\\delta(t)$ samples $e^{-st}$ at $t=0$ and the lower limit includes the boundary value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\\\int_{0^-}^{\\\\infty}\\\\delta(t)e^{-st}dt=e^{-s\\cdot 0}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, $\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{5\\delta(t)\\}=5\\cdot 1 =5$, and the same pattern is $\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{A\\delta(t)\\}=A$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
